WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.053
Three public entry points make the uses concrete.

2
00:00:04.053 --> 00:00:11.083
The SDK connects named FPGA boards, configurations and software build and boot flows.

3
00:00:11.083 --> 00:00:16.129
Check the actual Genesys2, Agilex7 and configuration records.

4
00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:22.167
Basilisk is a specific silicon implementation based on Cheshire and CVA6.

5
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:26.137
Cheshire already contains a CVA6-based platform;

6
00:00:26.137 --> 00:00:32.258
the plus sign separates responsibilities rather than adding a second core.

7
00:00:32.258 --> 00:00:36.724
The project authors provide Linux-boot demonstrations,

8
00:00:36.724 --> 00:00:40.777
silicon measurements and implementation material.

9
00:00:40.777 --> 00:00:47.891
Those records concern that system; they do not assign its frequency or power to yours.

10
00:00:47.891 --> 00:00:53.681
Open implementation also does not mean every physical asset is public:

11
00:00:53.681 --> 00:00:56.989
the GDS download omits proprietary pads.

12
00:00:56.989 --> 00:01:00.463
Occamy shows a different division of work.

13
00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:07.080
CVA6 manages a Linux system, while Snitch clusters handle intensive computation.

14
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.050
Four squares are a group icon, not a core count.

15
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.186
Scheduling and control describe a management role,

16
00:01:15.186 --> 00:01:20.231
not a demonstrated hardware scheduler or real-time guarantee.

17
00:01:20.231 --> 00:01:23.457
Board drawings and bars are conceptual.

18
00:01:23.457 --> 00:01:29.247
The course has not independently reproduced the boots or measurements;

19
00:01:29.247 --> 00:01:33.465
the Basilisk footer date is a research access date.

20
00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:36.939
Choose evidence relevant to your question.

21
00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:43.060
Next, distinguish these system records from future development directions.

